big: Leviticus
lede: Its most famous verses are three. Its longest arguments are about debt, land, wages and the edge of the harvest, and an empire that cites it has to keep those chapters out of general circulation.
calls:
k: Why a full dossier
The audit gives it a dossier on two grounds: the body treated as a jurisdiction, and the economic and restorative demands Dominion suppresses. Both are here, at the length they need.
||
k: How to work it
Reference is about a third of these pages, and the rest is yours. Four pages carry content notes. Skip them if you need to, and the dossier still works.
/end

@page rh="LEVITICUS · 1 OF 15 · THE BOOK ON THE RECORD" m="LEV 1.1; LEV 1–16; LEV 17–26; LEV 27; LEV 25.32–33; MILGROM, LEVITICUS 1–16; TKC: DOM"
# What it is
rec:
Name | English from the Greek and Latin, "about the Levites," though Levites as a group appear only once (25.32–33). Hebrew *Vayikra*, "and he called," the opening word. The Hebrew title names an address, and the Greek names a professional class.
Place | Third book of the Torah, set entirely at Sinai. The tabernacle has just been built at the end of Exodus, and Leviticus is what's said from inside it.
Authorship | Traditionally Moses. CONTESTED: Modern scholarship generally treats chapters 1–16 and 17–26 as separate bodies of priestly material, the second called the Holiness Code since August Klostermann named it in 1877. Dates and order are debated.
Structure | 1–7, offerings. 8–10, the ordination of the priests and its first disaster. 11–15, clean and unclean. 16, the Day of Atonement. 17–26, holiness, sexuality, festivals, land, debt and release. 27, vows and valuations.
Genre | Ritual instruction, addressed mostly to priests and sometimes to everyone. It's a procedures manual. Read it the way you'd read a professional standard.
A warning | More people quote this book than have read it. INTERPRETATION: Any argument built from Leviticus that can't say what chapter 25 requires is quoting selectively.
box: What the book is actually about
INTERPRETATION: Proximity. What it costs to live near a holy thing, who's admitted, who's put outside and for how long, and what has to be given back before anyone can come near again. Holiness here is a spatial and economic arrangement.
/end
prompt: What I was taught Leviticus was for, and which verses I was taught from it | fill

@page rh="LEVITICUS · 2 OF 15 · WHAT THE OFFERINGS DO" m="LEV 1–3; LEV 4.1–5.13; LEV 5.14–6.7; MILGROM, LEVITICUS 1–16; TKC: DOM · SANCTUM"
# A system with a sliding scale
Five main offerings, each with its own occasion, procedure and disposal of the parts. Two are for wrongdoing and three aren't. The scale matters. The same rite can be a bull, a bird or a measure of flour, depending on what the worshipper has. Access is priced to income, and the text says so.
tabrl:
| Offering | What it is for | Built-in scale |
| Burnt · *ʿolah* | The whole animal burned, the standing offering of the sanctuary | A bull, a sheep or goat, or birds |
| Grain · *minḥah* | Flour, oil and frankincense. A handful is burned and the rest goes to the priests, with salt, "the salt of the covenant" (2.13). | Cooked or raw, in several forms |
| Well-being · *shelamim* | A shared meal. The fat is burned, and the worshipper's household eats the meat. | Herd or flock |
| Purification · *ḥattaʾt*, traditionally "sin offering" | Its blood purges the sanctuary of the impurity a person's error or condition has caused | Graded by status, down to two birds, and down to a tenth of an ephah of flour for someone who can't afford birds |
| Reparation · *ʾasham*, traditionally "guilt offering" | For misusing holy things and defrauding a neighbor under oath | The full amount restored to the wronged person, plus a fifth, before the ram is offered |
box: The part no empire preaches
INTERPRETATION: In the reparation law, ritual doesn't stand in for repayment. You can't settle a defrauded neighbor at the altar. The neighbor is paid first, with a penalty added, and only then is the offering brought. A religion that offers forgiveness without restitution has reversed this chapter.
/end
prompt: What Dominion charges for absolution, and who is paid, if anyone | fill

@page rh="LEVITICUS · 3 OF 15 · CLEAN, UNCLEAN AND OUTSIDE" m="LEV 11; LEV 13–14; LEV 13.45–46; DOUGLAS, PURITY AND DANGER (1966); DOUGLAS, LEVITICUS AS LITERATURE (1999); KLAWANS, IMPURITY AND SIN"
note: Disease, bodily discharge and exclusion from the community, including quarantine applied to people.
# The body as jurisdiction
Impurity in Leviticus is a status, and mostly not a moral one. It limits access to the sanctuary, it's picked up through ordinary events, birth, discharge, illness, contact with a corpse, and it ends with time, washing and sometimes an offering. Translating it as "dirty" or "shameful" brings in a judgment the system doesn't make.
rec:
What it regulates | Who may approach, when and after what interval. Almost everyone is impure at some point, priests included, and priests live under stricter rules about corpses. It's a calendar of access more than a register of character.
Two kinds | Jonathan Klawans distinguishes ritual impurity, unavoidable and not sinful, from moral impurity, which comes from grave sins like sexual transgression, idolatry and bloodshed, defiles the land and can't be washed off. The later Christian split into moral and ceremonial law maps onto neither neatly.
The skin chapters · 13–14 | *Tsaraʿat* can appear on skin, on cloth and on the walls of houses. It isn't leprosy in the modern sense, though the Greek *lepra* carried that name into English. A priest inspects, isolates for seven days, inspects again and declares. Someone declared unclean tears their clothes, leaves their hair loose, covers their upper lip, calls out "Unclean, unclean" and lives alone outside the camp. Readmission is as detailed as exclusion.
Dietary law · 11 | Animals sorted by stated criteria, split hooves and chewing the cud, fins and scales, never explained. Hygiene, separation from neighbors and Mary Douglas's anomaly reading have all been proposed. None is settled, and the only reason given is holiness.
box: Where the danger is for a writer
INTERPRETATION: A status system with an inspector, an interval and a public declaration is the most transferable machinery in the Torah. Dominion doesn't need to invent contamination law. It only has to attach moral weight to a status this book keeps mostly non-moral.
/end
prompt: Who inspects bodies in Dominion, and what they're authorized to declare | fill

@page rh="LEVITICUS · 4 OF 15 · BIRTH, BLOOD AND ACCESS" m="LEV 12; LEV 15.1–18; LEV 15.19–33; LUKE 2.22–24; MARK 5.25–34; MILGROM, LEVITICUS 1–16; TKC: DOM"
note: Menstruation, childbirth and the regulation of women's bodies by religious law.
# Chapter 12 and chapter 15
Two chapters govern access after childbirth and during discharges, for women and for men. Read them together. The male provisions are there, and they're usually left out of any sermon on the female ones.
rec:
After a birth | Impure seven days after a son, then thirty-three more before she may touch holy things or enter the sanctuary. For a daughter both numbers double, to fourteen and sixty-six. The text gives no reason. Every explanation you've heard is interpretation.
The offering after | A lamb and a bird. If she can't afford a lamb, two birds will do (12.8). The concession is written into the law, which means the law expects poor women to use it. Luke records Mary bringing the poorer offering (Luke 2.22–24).
Men in chapter 15 | Matching sections for men and women: abnormal male discharge, emission, intercourse, menstruation and abnormal female bleeding. A man with an abnormal discharge brings the same two birds as a woman with abnormal bleeding. After intercourse both are impure until evening. The chapter states its purpose, to keep the people from defiling the tabernacle among them (15.31).
What it isn't | These states aren't punishments or sins. They're conditions with a procedure. INTERPRETATION: Whether the system still weighs more heavily on women in practice is a fair question, and a separate one from what the text calls it.
Reception | Later Jewish law developed these rules into a detailed practice with its own debates, and churches developed their own customs about women after childbirth. Don't read either back into Leviticus. The woman with bleeding who touches Jesus' clothes lives under 15.25–30 (Mark 5).
box: The useful question for TKC
INTERPRETATION: The question is who runs the calendar. An empire that decides when a woman may enter a building has an instrument for scheduling her whole life, and can attach a fee to it.
/end
prompt: What my world does with a woman's body and a calendar | fill

@page rh="LEVITICUS · 5 OF 15 · THE DAY OF ATONEMENT" m="LEV 10.1–9; LEV 16; LEV 16.16; LEV 16.29; LEV 23.26–32; 1 ENOCH 8–10; HEB 9 (RECEPTION); MILGROM, LEVITICUS 1–16; TKC: SANCTUM"
# One day, two goats
Once a year the high priest enters the innermost space, after washings and changes of clothes, and the sanctuary itself is cleansed of a year's accumulated contamination. Two goats are chosen by lot. One is offered. Over the other the high priest confesses the people's sins with both hands on its head, and it's sent alive into the wilderness. The day applies to the resident foreigner as well as the native.
rec:
What's being cleansed | The building, first. Blood is sprinkled to cleanse the sanctuary "because of the uncleannesses of the people of Israel and their transgressions" (16.16). Sin has a physical residue here, and it collects where God is said to live. The people's atonement follows.
The goat that leaves | The second goat is "for Azazel." CONTESTED: Tyndale coined "scapegoat" in 1530, reading "the goat that departs." Others propose a place name for a rugged cliff, a wilderness demon (Azazel is a fallen angel in 1 Enoch), or an abstract noun for complete removal. Don't read the modern social meaning of "scapegoat" back into the verse.
Confession aloud | The high priest confesses on behalf of the whole people, in public, and the assembly does no work and denies itself. A national accounting on a fixed date, performed by the highest office in everyone's hearing.
Two priests died in chapter 10 | The chapter opens "after the death of the two sons of Aaron." Nadab and Abihu offered fire they weren't commanded to offer, and fire consumed them. Aaron was silent and was forbidden to mourn in public. The system that follows was written by people for whom improvising at the altar was lethal.
Reception | Later Jewish tradition made Yom Kippur the year's central day of repentance without a temple, and Hebrews reads the chapter through Christ. Both are receptions of Leviticus 16.
prompt: Dominion's annual accounting. Who confesses, who's sent out, and who watches | fill

@page rh="LEVITICUS · 6 OF 15 · CHAPTERS 18 AND 20" m="LEV 18; LEV 20; GEN 43.32; DEUT 14.3; DEUT 25.16; PROV 6.16–19; GAGNON (2001); OLYAN (1994); MILGROM, LEVITICUS 17–22"
note: Sexual prohibitions, incest, capital penalties, and the modern use of two verses against gay people. The page reports the text and the state of the argument and doesn't settle your theology for you.
# The two verses and the rest of the list
Chapter 18 is addressed to Israelite men and opens by refusing the practices of Egypt and Canaan. Most of it is forbidden unions with kin, set out from the man's side. A man's own daughter isn't named, which scholars have explained in different ways. Chapter 20 assigns penalties, often death.
rec:
What's in the list | Close kin by blood and marriage, a wife's sister as a rival while the wife lives, sex during menstruation, a neighbor's wife, giving children to Molech, lying with a male "as with a woman," and sex with animals, the only verse that makes a woman the one acting.
"Abomination" | *Toʿevah* covers the whole list. It's used elsewhere for unclean food, dishonest weights and a lying tongue. The English carries a revulsion the Hebrew may not.
The selection test | INTERPRETATION: Anyone citing chapter 18 as binding is also citing the menstruation clause, and is standing seven chapters from the debt release. Consistency is a question about the reader.
tabrl:
| CONTESTED: 18.22 and 20.13 | What it holds | Associated with |
| A general prohibition | All sexual relations between men, carried forward in the New Testament (Rom 1, 1 Cor 6) | Robert Gagnon (2001), and most historic church teaching |
| A specific act | The phrasing points narrowly to anal intercourse between men | Saul Olyan (1994). Milgrom also limited it to that act and to Israelite men in the land. |
| The Code's own concerns | Read inside a system organized around lineage, seed and category boundaries | Various, building on Douglas |
| Applicability | Whether the Holiness Code binds Christians at all, and by what principle some laws are kept | The moral, ceremonial and civil division, and its critics |
prompt: Which verses Dominion made statute, and which it left as scripture | fill

@page rh="LEVITICUS · 7 OF 15 · CHAPTER 19, THE NEIGHBOR CHAPTER" m="LEV 19.2–3; LEV 19.9–10; LEV 19.13–18; LEV 19.20–22; LEV 19.33–36; MARK 12.31; RUTH 2; MILGROM, LEVITICUS 17–22"
# The chapter nobody quotes whole
"Love your neighbor as yourself" sits in the middle of a chapter of labor law, harvest law, court procedure and protection for disabled people. In context it's the summary clause of a list of concrete obligations. The chapter opens "You shall be holy, for I the LORD your God am holy" and puts ritual and ethics side by side without ranking them. Revere your mother and father, mother first.
rec:
The harvest edge | Don't reap to the border of the field, don't strip the vineyard, leave what falls for the poor and the foreigner. A standing claim on private production, collected by the poor themselves. Ruth gleans under this law.
Wages the same day | A hired worker's pay may not be held overnight. Wage theft is named beside robbery.
Disability | Don't curse the deaf or put a stumbling block before the blind, harms the victim can't see coming or answer. Compare chapter 21, the same book with a different logic.
Courts and speech | No partiality to great or poor, no slander, and don't stand by while your neighbor's blood is shed. Reprove openly instead of hating in your heart. Confrontation is commanded as the alternative to a grudge.
The foreigner | Treated as a native, "and you shall love him as yourself, for you were foreigners in Egypt." The famous command appears twice, and the second time it's about immigrants.
Honest weights | Just balances, weights and measures. Commercial fraud closes the chapter that holds the love command.
box: An enslaved woman, 19.20–22
Content note. A man who lies with an enslaved woman promised to another man isn't put to death, "because she was not free." There's an inquiry or a penalty (the word is disputed), and he brings a reparation offering. The text says nothing about her.
/end
prompt: Where the harvest edge would be in my world, and who fenced it | fill

@page rh="LEVITICUS · 8 OF 15 · CHAPTER 25, THE SUPPRESSED CHAPTER" m="LEV 25.1–7; LEV 25.8–17; LEV 25.23–28; LEV 25.35–46; NEH 10.31; 1 MACC 6.49, 53; BERGSMA, THE JUBILEE FROM LEVITICUS TO QUMRAN; TKC: DOM · MEN"
# Land, debt and release
Every seventh year the land rests. In the fiftieth year, by proclamation, holdings go back to the families that lost them and people in debt service go home. The stated ground is ownership. Land isn't sold permanently because it isn't the seller's to sell, "for the land is mine, and you are foreigners and sojourners with me" (25.23).
rec:
The sabbath year | No sowing, no reaping, no harvesting the vines. What grows feeds the household, the workers, the hired laborer, the resident foreigner, the livestock and the wild animals alike.
The fiftieth year | Proclaimed with a horn on the Day of Atonement: liberty throughout the land. The economic reset is announced on the day of national confession, which ties the two together on purpose.
Price mechanics | Sale prices are counted by the number of harvests left until the release. What changes hands is a lease of crops more than the land itself. Debt is designed to expire. Houses in walled cities are an exception.
No interest | Take no interest from a poor kinsman, and support him so he can live beside you. One who sells himself is to be treated as a hired worker until the Jubilee.
Redemption duty | A kinsman may and should buy back a relative's land or freedom. Rescue is a family obligation with a legal form, the machinery Ruth runs on.
The boundary in it | The protections are for kin. The same chapter lets Israel buy slaves from the nations and from resident foreigners, pass them on as inheritance and "make them slaves forever" (25.44–46). Print it whenever the Jubilee is praised.
Was it practiced | Sabbath years are attested in the Second Temple period. There's no clear record of a Jubilee being kept. CONTESTED: Whether it was enacted law, a utopian program or an ideal invoked now and then.
box: Why an empire can't circulate this chapter
INTERPRETATION: A permanent land market and inheritable debt are the two ways a household gets captured for generations. Chapter 25 forbids both among kin and grounds the ban in the deliverance the Empire also claims. It can't be denied and can't be taught, so it goes to the closed catechism.
/end
prompt: Which household in my cast a Jubilee would free, and who would lose by it | fill

@page rh="LEVITICUS · 9 OF 15 · THE CLOSED CATECHISM · WRITING" m="WRITING; LEV 19; LEV 25; TKC: DOM · SANCTUM"
# Build the suppression
Dominion can't delete a chapter its own scripture contains. It has to manage it: restrict it, spiritualize it, postpone it, or attach conditions that never arrive. Draft the mechanism here.
