@page rh="LEVITICUS · 10 OF 15 · WHO MAY APPROACH" m="LEV 21.1–15; LEV 21.16–24; LEV 22.10–16; LEV 22.20–25; LEV 19.14; ISA 56.3–5; OLYAN, DISABILITY IN THE HEBREW BIBLE (2008); TKC: TR"
# The body of the official
Chapter 21 limits who may serve at the altar by descent, marriage, mourning and body. A priest with a listed condition may eat the holy food and may not officiate. The rule concerns how the sanctuary presents itself, and it shuts a class of people out of an office because of their bodies. Both halves of that sentence belong on the page.
rec:
The list | A man who's blind or lame, has a marked face or a limb too long, a broken foot or hand, a hunched back, dwarfism, a defect of the eye, scabs or crushed testicles. The same word, *mum*, marks animals unfit for sacrifice. Interpreters note the symmetry between offerer, offering and officiant, which explains the logic without justifying it.
Held against chapter 19 | The book that protects the deaf and the blind from harm bars them from the priesthood. It's a real tension inside Leviticus, and the honest move is to name it. Isaiah 56 later promises eunuchs a name in God's house better than sons and daughters.
Marriage and mourning | Limits on whom a priest may marry, tighter for the high priest, and on which dead he may mourn. A priest's daughter who becomes a prostitute is to be burned (21.9). Office reaches into the household and the funeral.
What priests live on | Set portions of the offerings, shared with their households and closed to outsiders. The sanctuary is also a payroll, and the text isn't embarrassed about it.
box: For Dominion
POSSIBLE: An empire choosing candidates for a public religious role has this chapter as precedent for physical screening, and the Trials are that kind of selection. Whether the Sanctum cites Leviticus 21 for its examinations is a plot decision.
/end
prompt: What disqualifies a body from service in my world, and who wrote the list | fill

