@page rh="LEVITICUS · 13 OF 15 · WHAT WAS DONE WITH IT" m="ISA 61.1–2; LUKE 4.16–21; ACTS 15; AQUINAS, ST I–II Q. 99; WESTMINSTER CONFESSION (1646) 19; BERGSMA, THE JUBILEE"
# A book used in two directions
Everything here is reception, what people did with Leviticus.
rec:
Inside the Bible | The release language of chapter 25 comes back in Isaiah 61, and Luke has Jesus read that passage in the synagogue at Nazareth and say it's fulfilled today (Luke 4.16–21). The Jubilee is quoted as a program.
Jewish practice | Rabbinic Judaism developed the purity, dietary and agricultural laws into living practice and argument, sabbatical-year observance included. Treating Leviticus as a dead text is a Christian habit.
Early Christian use | In Acts 15 the church decided which of these laws applied to non-Jewish believers. Thomas Aquinas later divided the law into moral, ceremonial and judicial precepts, and the Westminster Confession (1646) made the division Reformed doctrine. The text doesn't contain it, and it's how chapter 18 is kept and chapter 25 dropped.
The Liberty Bell | Cast for the Pennsylvania State House in 1752 and recast in 1753 with 25.10 on it. Abolitionists adopted it in the 1830s. The *Anti-Slavery Record* called it the Liberty Bell in 1835, and a poem in Garrison's *Liberator* in 1839 spread the name.
Debt campaigns | The Jubilee 2000 campaign for cancelling the debts of the poorest countries took its name and its argument from chapter 25.
Purity as insult | "Unclean" and "leper" have long been used outside the sanctuary system for people a community wants to keep out, by race, disease or sexuality. The most consequential misuse of the book.
corr: The old companion said the word "jubilee" is on the bell. It isn't. The bell reads "Proclaim LIBERTY Throughout all the Land unto all the Inhabitants Thereof," with the reference Lev. XXV. v X. Its debt reading also needed fixing: the Jubilee returns land and frees Israelites in debt service, and the seventh-year debt release is Deuteronomy 15.
prompt: Where I've heard purity language used about a person | fill

@page rh="LEVITICUS · 14 OF 15 · DOMINION'S LEVITICUS" m="LEV 19.9–15; LEV 25; LEV 6.1–7; TKC: DOM · SANCTUM"
# The half the Empire teaches
POSSIBLE: Everything on this page is a TKC construction to test against the manuscript.
tabrl:
| What the Sanctum circulates | What it keeps in the closed catechism |
| Holiness as personal purity, and the sexual prohibitions as household law | Chapter 25 entire. Land returned, debts ended, servitude ended by proclamation. |
| Love your neighbor, as a disposition | The harvest edge, the same-day wage and the ban on interest that define what the love requires |
| Inspection, quarantine and readmission as public health and public order | Impurity in the source text is temporary, recurring, mostly non-moral, and applies to priests too |
| Atonement as an annual act of submission, scheduled by the Sanctum | The rite cleanses the sanctuary of what the institution itself has accumulated |
| Restitution as an offering paid to the Temple | The defrauded person is paid first, in full, plus a fifth, before any offering counts |
box: The argument in one paragraph
INTERPRETATION: Dominion doesn't need to falsify Leviticus. It teaches chapters 11 through 20 and shelves 19.9–15 and chapter 25, keeping the machinery of exclusion and dropping the machinery of release. A character who reads the whole book finds the Empire's own scripture arguing against it, a stronger plot device than any invented heresy.
/end
prompt: Who in my cast would rather not have heard chapter 25 read aloud | fill

